1
00:00:03,291 --> 00:00:07,110
At 01:23 on 26 April 1986,

2
00:00:07,561 --> 00:00:10,899
Reactor Four of the Chernobyl
nuclear plant exploded.

3
00:00:12,271 --> 00:00:14,378
It caused the largest
uncontrolled release

4
00:00:14,398 --> 00:00:17,959
of radioactivity ever recorded
from a civilian nuclear plant.

5
00:00:19,172 --> 00:00:22,398
This is what the record shows,
and what is still argued.

6
00:00:26,631 --> 00:00:30,651
Chernobyl lies in the north of Ukraine,
then part of the Soviet Union,

7
00:00:30,842 --> 00:00:33,418
about one hundred ten
kilometres north of Kyiv.

8
00:00:34,991 --> 00:00:39,152
The power complex had four reactors
of a Soviet design called the RBMK,

9
00:00:39,864 --> 00:00:41,639
and two more were
under construction.

10
00:00:42,931 --> 00:00:44,466
Beside it, a reservoir

11
00:00:44,497 --> 00:00:47,758
of twenty two square kilometres
had been built to cool the reactors.

12
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:51,448
Three kilometres away stood Pripyat,

13
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,619
a new city built for the plant's
workers and their families.

14
00:00:55,892 --> 00:00:58,097
About forty nine thousand
people lived there.

15
00:00:59,451 --> 00:01:04,638
The RBMK used graphite and boiling
water, and it carried a serious flaw.

16
00:01:06,071 --> 00:01:09,433
At low power, more steam made
the chain reaction stronger,

17
00:01:09,503 --> 00:01:11,299
and the reactor could
run out of control.

18
00:01:12,531 --> 00:01:15,529
A Soviet state committee found in 1991

19
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:19,059
that the plant's operators had not
been made aware of this danger.

20
00:01:20,291 --> 00:01:24,998
On 25 April, the reactor was due to
shut down for routine maintenance.

21
00:01:26,531 --> 00:01:29,158
Before it did, the crew planned a test

22
00:01:29,178 --> 00:01:31,283
of whether a slowing
turbine could still

23
00:01:31,303 --> 00:01:33,559
power the cooling pumps
during a blackout.

24
00:01:34,992 --> 00:01:37,858
The same test had been tried the
year before, and had not worked.

25
00:01:42,191 --> 00:01:44,608
At 14:00 on 25 April,

26
00:01:44,919 --> 00:01:48,138
the emergency cooling system
was switched off for the test.

27
00:01:49,691 --> 00:01:50,334
The same hour,

28
00:01:50,354 --> 00:01:53,698
the Kyiv grid controller asked
for the test to be postponed.

29
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:57,859
The reactor kept running at
reduced power into the night.

30
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:03,232
At 00:28, as the power
control was changed over,

31
00:02:03,584 --> 00:02:05,399
the power fell to almost nothing.

32
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:08,426
That was not in the test plan,

33
00:02:08,497 --> 00:02:11,698
and it left the reactor in an
extremely unstable condition.

34
00:02:13,111 --> 00:02:14,647
To raise the power again,

35
00:02:14,798 --> 00:02:17,599
the crew withdrew too
many of the control rods.

36
00:02:18,791 --> 00:02:22,490
By 01:03 the power was only
two hundred megawatts,

37
00:02:23,062 --> 00:02:25,819
far below the planned level,
and the test went ahead.

38
00:02:27,212 --> 00:02:30,059
Some of the automatic shutdown
systems had been disabled.

39
00:02:31,352 --> 00:02:34,539
At 01:23, the test began.

40
00:02:35,711 --> 00:02:37,086
Thirty six seconds later,

41
00:02:37,318 --> 00:02:39,959
an operator pressed the
emergency shutdown button.

42
00:02:41,231 --> 00:02:42,777
A peculiarity in the design

43
00:02:42,797 --> 00:02:45,918
of the control rods made the
power surge as they went in.

44
00:02:47,491 --> 00:02:51,521
In three seconds, the power passed
five hundred thirty megawatts,

45
00:02:51,541 --> 00:02:54,419
more than twice the level at
which the test had begun.

46
00:02:55,791 --> 00:03:00,058
There was a steam explosion, and two
or three seconds later, a second one.

47
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:03,006
The reactor was destroyed,

48
00:03:03,036 --> 00:03:06,959
and burning graphite and fragments
of fuel were thrown out across the site.

49
00:03:08,232 --> 00:03:11,359
Two workers died in the
explosions that night.

50
00:03:15,511 --> 00:03:18,851
Firefighters fought the fires on
the roof of the turbine building,

51
00:03:19,123 --> 00:03:20,998
and put them out within a few hours.

52
00:03:22,451 --> 00:03:26,283
Within weeks, six of those firemen
were among the people who had died

53
00:03:26,303 --> 00:03:28,038
of acute radiation sickness.

54
00:03:29,511 --> 00:03:33,058
The fire in the reactor core
burned for about ten days.

55
00:03:34,351 --> 00:03:36,789
Water was pumped into
the reactor for half a day,

56
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,498
then stopped, for fear of
flooding the other units.

57
00:03:41,071 --> 00:03:42,093
From the second day,

58
00:03:42,113 --> 00:03:45,491
helicopters dropped about
five thousand tonnes of boron,

59
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:49,139
dolomite, sand, clay
and lead onto the core.

60
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:52,760
One after another they carried them
out of here,

61
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:57,260
such sacks of boron, dolomite, lead,
sand, clay.

62
00:03:58,471 --> 00:04:02,438
On 27 April, the city of
Pripyat was evacuated.

63
00:04:03,831 --> 00:04:05,879
A loudspeaker
announcement told residents

64
00:04:05,899 --> 00:04:08,419
that buses would arrive
from two in the afternoon.

65
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:11,940
...a need arises

66
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:15,340
to carry out a temporary evacuation

67
00:04:15,390 --> 00:04:16,940
of the city's residents.

68
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:21,749
They were told to take documents,
essential things and food.

69
00:04:22,841 --> 00:04:26,769
About forty five thousand people
left, and Pripyat was never resettled.

70
00:04:27,861 --> 00:04:31,711
By 14 May, about one hundred
sixteen thousand people

71
00:04:31,731 --> 00:04:34,989
from within thirty kilometres of
the plant had been evacuated.

72
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:39,079
The wind carried radioactive
dust over Ukraine,

73
00:04:39,170 --> 00:04:42,269
Belarus and Russia,
and on across Europe.

74
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:44,945
By 28 April,

75
00:04:44,965 --> 00:04:46,770
monitoring stations in Poland

76
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:50,209
and across Scandinavia were
recording a radioactive cloud.

77
00:04:51,381 --> 00:04:55,289
On Italian television, the news anchor
reported what Moscow had said.

78
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:58,630
Moscow's authorities confirm

79
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,830
that, contrary to what has been said
in many quarters,

80
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:03,230
the accident has caused only two
deaths.

81
00:05:04,371 --> 00:05:06,057
Several countries called the lack

82
00:05:06,077 --> 00:05:09,339
of information from the Soviet
authorities irresponsible.

83
00:05:10,651 --> 00:05:12,745
About two hundred
thousand liquidators,

84
00:05:12,916 --> 00:05:14,810
from all over the Soviet Union,

85
00:05:15,041 --> 00:05:18,899
took part in the clean-up
in 1986 and 1987.

86
00:05:20,252 --> 00:05:23,339
Some cleared radioactive
debris from the roofs by hand.

87
00:05:24,551 --> 00:05:26,185
In a Soviet film of the time,

88
00:05:26,476 --> 00:05:28,753
physicists measured
the dose at each spot

89
00:05:29,064 --> 00:05:31,019
and set the time limit for each worker.

90
00:05:32,130 --> 00:05:34,780
Before these figures became known and
understood,

91
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:37,080
the roentgens, the minutes,

92
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,180
physicists had to go through
everywhere.

93
00:05:40,321 --> 00:05:43,313
Armoured machines and remote
controlled equipment were sent

94
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:44,888
to the most dangerous places.

95
00:05:46,210 --> 00:05:49,610
These specialised machines went in,

96
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:53,460
with armoured cabs lined with lead.

97
00:05:54,561 --> 00:05:56,710
The same film said that
the danger remained

98
00:05:56,730 --> 00:05:59,009
that the core might melt
down into the ground.

99
00:06:03,201 --> 00:06:04,395
Within seven months,

100
00:06:04,827 --> 00:06:08,149
a concrete shelter was built
around the ruined reactor.

101
00:06:09,361 --> 00:06:13,008
It was built in haste, and designed
to last only twenty to thirty years.

102
00:06:14,742 --> 00:06:19,969
In 2016, a steel arch one hundred ten
metres high was slid over it on rails.

103
00:06:21,261 --> 00:06:25,808
The last working reactor at Chernobyl
was shut down in December 2000.

104
00:06:27,281 --> 00:06:29,508
Workers and their families
moved to a new town,

105
00:06:29,528 --> 00:06:32,969
Slavutich, thirty
kilometres from the plant.

106
00:06:34,281 --> 00:06:38,289
Officially, thirty plant workers and
firemen died within three months.

107
00:06:39,621 --> 00:06:40,352
After that,

108
00:06:40,703 --> 00:06:44,671
United Nations scientists linked
about five thousand thyroid cancers

109
00:06:44,691 --> 00:06:45,543
to the accident,

110
00:06:45,874 --> 00:06:48,670
and fifteen of those
were fatal by 2005.

111
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:53,448
They found no clear evidence
of a major rise in other cancers.

112
00:06:54,801 --> 00:06:57,616
Estimates of the eventual
death toll still differ widely:

113
00:06:57,866 --> 00:07:00,692
a United Nations report
predicted about four thousand,

114
00:07:00,712 --> 00:07:04,529
and in 2006 Greenpeace estimated
more than ninety thousand.

115
00:07:06,182 --> 00:07:09,848
The first official account, in
1986, blamed the operators.

116
00:07:11,181 --> 00:07:14,403
In 1992, an international
group put more weight

117
00:07:14,513 --> 00:07:15,968
on the design of the reactor.

118
00:07:17,401 --> 00:07:22,703
Valery Legasov, who led the Soviet
delegation to the IAEA review,

119
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:25,649
wrote that it was impossible
to find a single culprit.

120
00:07:26,921 --> 00:07:30,081
Caesium one thirty seven, one
of the main contaminants,

121
00:07:30,432 --> 00:07:32,288
has a half life of thirty years.

122
00:07:33,882 --> 00:07:37,469
About a thousand people returned to
live in the zone without permission.

123
00:07:38,621 --> 00:07:41,438
In 2011, the zone was
opened to tourists,

124
00:07:41,669 --> 00:07:45,129
and visits peaked at about
one hundred thousand in 2019.

125
00:07:46,541 --> 00:07:51,148
In 2022, Russian forces took control
of the site for about five weeks.

126
00:07:52,681 --> 00:07:56,239
The Exclusion Zone began as
a circle thirty kilometres wide,

127
00:07:56,630 --> 00:08:00,169
and now covers about four thousand
three hundred square kilometres.

128
00:08:01,681 --> 00:08:03,456
The reactor was destroyed in seconds,

129
00:08:03,928 --> 00:08:06,988
but the argument over who was
to blame has lasted decades.

130
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:11,467
If the flaw was known, who
should have told the operators?
